WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:48.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In 1928, a strange phenomenon was sweeping the state of Idaho, a vanishing act of sorts. A Boise resident would wake up on a typical Monday morning, drag themselves out of bed, get dressed for the day, and they would hop in their car to drive to work, not noticing that something was missing. And suddenly, cops in the rear view. And the next thing that the driver of the car knew, they were getting picked up because they weren't displaying a... license plate. Idaho historian Rick Just says license plates, well, they were disappearing. The Secretary of State was fielding complaints about all the lost tags. And soon enough, the culprit became clear. Tourists would come to Idaho and steal the plates.
这里是 99% Invisible。我是罗曼·马尔斯。1928年，爱达荷州正在席卷一股奇怪的现象，某种程度上的消失行为。一个博伊西居民在一个平常的周一早晨醒来，把自己从床上拖起来，穿好衣服准备出门，他们会跳上车去上班，却没有注意到有什么东西不见了。突然间，后视镜里出现了警察。车里的司机接下来意识到的事情是，他们被拦下了，因为他们没有悬挂……车牌。爱达荷州历史学家里克·贾斯特说，车牌，嗯，它们正在消失。州务卿不断接到关于所有丢失车牌的投诉。不久之后，罪魁祸首就水落石出了。游客来到爱达荷州，偷走了这些车牌。

2
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Idaho's license plates were being snatched up like plush hotel bathrobes. Yeah, yeah. People would come up, but they would pull up to either a tourist park or a motel or something, and they would spot those plates and think, you know, I'd like to have a souvenir. and so they would just take it off of the car and take it home. And there was a reason why people couldn't resist swiping Idaho plates in particular. That year, the state had revolutionized license plate design. That's reporter Daniel Ackerman. Before this, plates were basic, with info like the state name and the registration numbers.
爱达荷州的车牌就像高档酒店的浴袍一样被抢走。是的，是的。人们会开车过来，把车停在旅游公园、汽车旅馆或类似的地方，然后他们会盯上那些车牌，心想，你知道的，我想要个纪念品。于是他们就会把车牌从车上卸下来带回家。人们对爱达荷州的车牌尤其无法抗拒，这是有原因的。那一年，该州彻底革新了车牌设计。那是记者丹尼尔·阿克曼。在此之前，车牌都很基础，上面只有州名和注册号等信息。

3
00:01:25.000 --> 00:02:05.000
All of this on a pretty simple, solid colored background. But in 1928, the Secretary of State in Idaho, had an epiphany. He was like, we have this half square foot of open real estate just rolling around on everyone's cars. Let's do something with it. The 1928 plate is often said to be the very first advertising license plate in the country. They're the very first one that tried to advertise a product. And the product that Idaho chose will surprise absolutely no one. The state's 1928 license plates all featured a single giant potato. A big kind of elongated, goofy-looking potato. The state of but big. It was almost as big as the plate.
所有这些都在一个相当简单、纯色的背景上。但在1928年，爱达荷州务卿突然有了一个顿悟。他当时想，我们每个人的车上都有这半平方英尺的空白地产在闲置。我们用它做点什么吧。1928年的车牌通常被认为是全国第一个广告车牌。它们是第一个试图宣传产品的车牌。而爱达荷州选择的产品绝对会让所有人大吃一惊。该州1928年的所有车牌上都印着一个巨大的土豆。一个很大、有点呈椭圆形、滑稽的土豆。这个州的——但很大。它几乎和车牌一样大。

4
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:36.000
The registration numbers were stamped in green lettering right on top of this lumpy brown spud. I would say it looks almost fecal in nature. It does. The shape, particularly, yes, yes. The execution wasn't perfect, but it was innovative. Below the tremendous tater, there was even a modest, pragmatic slogan. Idaho potatoes. Today, every state's got a marketing slogan. Hawaii is the Aloha State, Missouri is the Show Me State.
注册号码直接用绿色字样印在这个凹凸不平的棕色土豆顶部。我要说它看起来几乎带有粪便的性质。确实如此。尤其是形状，是的，是的。制作过程并不完美，但它很有创新性。在这块巨大的土豆下方，甚至还有一个朴素、务实的口号。爱达荷土豆。今天，每个州都有营销口号。夏威夷是阿罗哈州，密苏里州是展示州。

5
00:02:36.000 --> 00:03:01.000
But Idaho was the first to put theirs on a license plate. They wanted to make sure that when people thought about Idaho, they thought potatoes. And for better or worse, the association stuck. And now it's gotten to be such a thing. I mean, coming up here in a few days at New Year's Eve, we drop a potato. What do you mean? Well, you know how the Times Square. comes floating down like that.
但爱达荷州是第一个把口号印在车牌上的州。他们想确保当人们想到爱达荷州时，就会想到土豆。不管好坏，这个联系就这么固定下来了。现在它已经成了一件大事。我的意思是，几天后的跨年夜，我们要举行掉土豆仪式。你是什么意思？嗯，你知道时代广场那个……

6
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Well, we have a big potato that comes floating down. They've been doing it about 10 years now. The concept of slapping a tagline onto a license plate might not seem like a big deal, but it turns out this idea would end up having outsized consequences and not just for Idaho. We're talking legislative clashes, multiple Supreme Court cases, and even jail time.
嗯，我们有一个大土豆落下来。他们已经搞了大约10年了。把标语贴在车牌上的概念可能看起来不算什么大事，但事实证明，这个想法最终会产生巨大的后果，而且不仅仅是对爱达荷州而言。我们在说的是立法冲突、多起最高法院案件，甚至是牢狱之灾。

7
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Because what started in one state would soon spread, and when it did, the question of what should, go on a license plate and what shouldn't would prove surprisingly contentious. The first state-issued license plates appeared at the very beginning of the 20th century, and they served a mostly bureaucratic function. More people were buying and crashing cars every year, so state governments originally mandated plates as a way to keep track of all the nuts behind the wheel. No one was interested in sloganeering.
因为最初在一个州开始的事情很快就会蔓延开来，当它蔓延时，车牌上应该出现什么、不应该出现什么的问题被证明具有惊人的争议性。全美第一个由州发行的车牌出现在20世纪初，它们主要用于行政职能。每年都有越来越多的人买车并发生车祸，因此州政府最初强制要求使用车牌，作为追踪方向盘后面所有马大哈的一种方式。当时没有人对标语感兴趣。

8
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:32.000
But then Americans discovered the road trip. Well, the big factor with increase in automobiles was that it allowed people freedom to roam. You could go wherever you wanted. Christine Byron is a former history librarian at the Grand Rapids Public Library. She focuses on the history of tourism, and she says that the rise of the road trip in the 1920s created this huge new tourist market. Drivers needed services like gas stations and roadside motels that had to be. existed in the age of steam-powered travel.
但随后美国人发现了自驾游。嗯，汽车增多带来的大因素是它让人们获得了四处游荡的自由。你想去哪儿就去哪儿。克里斯蒂娜·拜伦是大急流城公共图书馆的前历史图书管理员。她专注于旅游史，她说20世纪20年代自驾游的兴起创造了一个巨大的全新旅游市场。司机需要加油站和路边汽车旅馆等服务，而这些在蒸汽动力旅行时代是不存在的。

9
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Travel in east, travel in west. When you traveled by steamship or railroad, you pretty much brought what you needed with you, and your meals were served at a resort. But once the automobile came along, there was a lot more money that needed to be spent. From the state's perspective, all those new tourist dollars were up for grabs.
向东旅行，向西旅行。当你乘蒸汽船或铁路旅行时，你基本上随身携带你需要的东西，你的饭菜则在度假村里供应。但一旦汽车出现，就需要花更多的钱。从州的视角来看，所有这些新的游客美元都唾手可得。

10
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:43.000
So states started letting the world know what they had to offer. Arizona had the Grand Canyon, Minnesota, its lakes. New Mexico, its average 310 days of sunshine per year. And in this war for tourists, states promoted themselves anywhere they could. National magazines, various automobile guides, the blue guide, the green book, and of course, tons and tons of promotional brochures. But no one thought to advertise on a license plate until 1928 when Idahoans realized that their plan. plates were too valuable to waste on just a registration number. And Rick Just says once Idaho staked its starchy flag on the license plate, the rush was on. License plates became a different thing because of that potato.
因此，各州开始向世界展示他们所拥有的东西。亚利桑那州拥有大峡谷，明尼苏达州拥有其湖泊。新墨西哥州平均每年有310天阳光普照。在这场争夺游客的战争中，各州尽可能地宣传自己。全国性杂志、各种汽车指南、蓝色指南、绿色指南，当然还有成吨成吨的宣传册。但直到1928年，当爱达荷州人意识到他们的平淡车牌太珍贵了，不能只浪费在一个注册号上时，才有人想到在车牌上做广告。里克·贾斯特说，一旦爱达荷州在车牌上插上了它那含有淀粉的旗帜，热潮就开始了。因为那个土豆，车牌变成了完全不同的东西。

11
00:05:43.000 --> 00:06:17.000
States spent the middle of the century transforming their plates from austere government documents into colorful boosters of tourism and industry. You could even think of them as miniature little ads that are driving, you know, all over the state and all over the country, hopefully. In 1940, Arizona stamped Grand Canyon State on its plates and never looked back. In 1950, Minnesota went with land of 10,000 lakes. Meanwhile, New Mexico actually put Sunshine State on its plates in 1932, before Florida muscled in the slogan in 1949.
在世纪中叶，各州花时间将车牌从简朴的政府文件转变为旅游业和工业的多彩宣传品。你甚至可以把它们想象成微型小广告，它们正在全国各地，希望如此，也在整个州到处行驶。1940年，亚利桑那州在车牌上印上了“大峡谷州”，并且再也没有回头。1950年，明尼苏达州选择了“万湖之州”。与此同时，新墨西哥州实际上在1932年就在车牌上印上了“阳光州”，比佛罗里达州在1949年抢占这个标语还要早。

12
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Florida, for the record, only has an average of 237 days of sunshine for year, but whatever. Wisconsin was America's dairy land. Maine was vacation land. Other states couldn't make up their minds. Michigan's plate, for example, initially sported the phrase water wonderland in 1954. Which then evolved into winter water wonderland. Followed by Great Lakes State, Great Lakes, and Great Lakes Splendor. In 1970, Michigan's state tourism council actually adopted the slogan, the Michigan's, the almost islands of the Great Lakes.
作为记录，佛罗里达州平均每年只有237天的阳光，但管他呢。威斯康星州是美国的乳酪之乡。缅因州是度假之乡。其他州则无法下定决心。例如，密歇根州的车牌最初在1954年印有“水上仙境”字样。后来演变成“冬水仙境”。接着是“五大湖州”、“五大湖”和“五大湖风光”。1970年，密歇根州旅游委员会实际上采用了“密歇根州，五大湖的准岛屿”这一口号。

13
00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:27.000
But sadly, that plate never happened. Today, license plates like these are a national institution, and it's fun in a kitsy Americana kind of way. Each state is earnestly trying to put its best foot forward. So what could possibly be wrong here? It turns out quite a bit. Because as fun as some of these plates might have been, at half a square foot, a license plate is a small canvas. And when you have to pick one symbol to represent an entire state, you are not going to please everyone. And this has caused trouble from the get-go.
但遗憾的是，那块车牌从未真正出现过。今天，像这样的车牌已经成为一种国家制度，并且以一种俗气的美国风情让人感到有趣。每个州都在真诚地展现自己最好的一面。那么这里究竟会有什么问题呢？事实证明，问题还真不少。因为不管其中一些车牌有多有趣，在半平方英尺的面积上，车牌只是一块小画布。当你要挑选一个符号来代表整个州时，你不可能取悦所有人。这从一开始就惹出了麻烦。

14
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:56.000
In 1928, when Idaho unleashed the potato plates, it didn't go over all that well. People detested those license plates. Lots of Idahoans, it turned out, resented being associated with the state's cash crop. Particularly people from northern Idaho, because they don't grow potatoes up there. Really, it's a kind of a southeastern Idaho thing. Newspaper editorials called it an embarrassment. One headline actually read, Why bring that up?
1928年，当爱达荷州推出土豆车牌时，情况并不太妙。人们厌恶那些车牌。事实证明，许多爱达荷州人反感与该州的经济作物联系在一起。特别是来自爱达荷州北部的人，因为他们那里不种土豆。真的，这更像是爱达荷州东南部的事情。报纸社论称其为一种尴尬。一个头条新闻甚至写道：“为什么要提起这个？”

15
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:36.000
And probably it's a good thing that they just dropped the idea entirely and went back to numbers in 1929. No motto, no graphics, and certainly no potatoes. And license plate quarrels weren't unique to Idaho. Florida had to dump one of its plate designs after residents complained that the grapefruit with a stem attached looked more like a bomb. Massachusetts, meanwhile, tried to put a codfish right next to its state's name, only to be blamed by fishermen for a poor catch that year because the fish on the plate was swimming away from the word Massachusetts. These dustups over license plate design can seem like, well, small potatoes.
可能他们完全放弃这个想法并在1929年改回纯数字是一件好事。没有座右铭，没有图形，当然也没有土豆。而且车牌争端并非爱达荷州独有。佛罗里达州不得不废弃其中一款车牌设计，因为居民抱怨带柄的葡萄看起来更像一颗炸弹。与此同时，马萨诸塞州试图在州名旁边放一条鳕鱼，结果当年却遭到渔民的指责，称其导致当年的渔获量不佳，因为车牌上的鱼正在游离“马萨诸塞”这个词。这些围绕车牌设计的争执看起来像是……嗯，小菜一碟。

16
00:08:36.000 --> 00:09:20.000
But the fight over licensed plates was about to be taken to the next level, thanks to a politician named Meljum Thompson. In this critical battle for the survival of America, we shall not tolerate a no-win settlement. Thompson was a titan of New Hampshire politics in the 1970s. He served three terms as governor, and he was a conservative firebrand who hated Democrats. We must drive from the seats of power in the White House, Congress, and the State Department, all of the foul brood of commie lovers. Thompson had a lot of unorthodox ideas, including wanting to arm the New Hampshire National Guard with nuclear weapons.
但由于一位名叫梅尔德里姆·汤普森的政治家，围绕车牌的斗争即将被推向一个新的高度。在这场关乎美国生存的关键战役中，我们绝不容忍没有赢家的妥协。汤普森是20世纪70年代新罕布什尔州政坛的泰斗。他担任过三届州长，是一个痛恨民主党的保守派狂热分子。我们必须把白宫、国会和国务院权力宝座上所有那些共产主义爱者的肮脏后代全部赶下台。汤普森有很多非传统的想法，包括希望用核武器武装新罕布什尔州国民警卫队。

17
00:09:20.000 --> 00:10:00.000
And he was obsessed with freedom. Here's Thompson's dorky campaign song. Live for your die, don't let the freedom pass you by, stand up proud and strong, and lead this country on. Live for your die, of course, is New Hampshire's fiery state motto. It was coined by a revolutionary war vet. And Thompson loved it so much that before he became governor, he worked with allies in the state legislature to get it slapped on every car in the state. And I don't know of any more prominent place to carry a message. then write on the license plate.
而且他对自由着迷。这是汤普森古怪的竞选歌曲。为自由而生或去死，不要让自由从你身边溜走，昂首挺胸，引领这个国家前进。为自由而生或去死，当然是新罕布什尔州激昂的州座右铭。它是由一位独立战争老兵创造的。汤普森非常喜欢它，以至于在成为州长之前，他与州议会的盟友合作，将其印在了州内每一辆汽车上。而且我不知道还有什么地方比直接在车牌上更能传递信息了。

18
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:42.000
That's the best billboard of all. In 1971, the slogan on the state's license plate changed from scenic New Hampshire to live free or die. But not everyone embraced the state's message. That's ridiculous. At 88 years old, George Maynard still gets heated about the New Hampshire license plate. And for good reason, it changed the. course of his life. George grew up in Rhode Island. He married a woman, Maxine, who he'd actually met in junior high. They settled into a pretty typical family life. They had kids. George got a job
那是最好的巨幅广告。1971年，该州车牌上的标语从“风景优美的新罕布什尔”改为“不自由，毋宁死”。但并非所有人都接受该州的信息。这太荒谬了。88岁的乔治·梅纳德谈起新罕布什尔州车牌时依然激愤不已。这是有充分理由的，它改变了。他的人生轨迹。乔治在罗德岛长大。他娶了一位名叫玛克辛的女子，两人其实在初中时就认识了。他们过上了相当典型的家庭生活。他们有了孩子。乔治找到了一份工作……

19
00:10:42.000 --> 00:11:23.000
as a newspaper printer. And then something happened, like really abruptly. In 1956, four years after I got married, the witnesses came to my house. They told me that God had a name and his name was Jehovah. George and his family joined the Jehovah's Witnesses. And by 1972, they had moved to Claremont, New Hampshire. That's where the trouble started. Every day, George would hop in his car and drive to work at the local printing press, with his New Hampshire license plates screaming, in all caps, live, free or die. And this really graded on George, because he didn't share Meldron Thompson's belief of freedom over everything.
作为一名报纸印刷工。然后发生了一件事，非常突然。1956年，在我结婚四年后，见证人来到了我家。他们告诉我上帝有个名字，他的名字叫耶和华。乔治和他的家人加入了耶和华见证人。到1972年，他们搬到了新罕布什尔州的克莱蒙特。麻烦就是从那里开始的。每天，乔治都会跳上车去当地的印刷厂上班，车上挂着的新罕布什尔州车牌用全部大写字母尖叫着“不自由，毋宁死”。这让乔治非常抓狂，因为他并不认同梅尔德里姆·汤普森关于自由高于一切的信念。

20
00:11:23.000 --> 00:12:02.000
As a Jehovah's Witness, George actually believed that God-given life was more important than freedom. Oh, that's right. Definitely. And the real existence of life is very precious. Life is a gift. And we appreciate it very much. George didn't want the government telling him what to die for. So then one day I decided, you know, if it's offensive, why should be forced to support something that's offensive? So I carved it up with writ tape. And when he erased the state motto, George marched to the front lines of the license plate wars. covering up the slogan was a violation of state law,
作为一名耶和华见证人，乔治实际上认为上帝赋予的生命比自由更重要。哦，没错。绝对的。而且生命真实的存在是非常宝贵的。生命是一份礼物。我们非常感激它。乔治不想让政府告诉他为什么去死。所以有一天我决定，你知道的，如果它具有冒犯性，为什么我要被迫支持某种具有冒犯性的东西呢？于是我用红色胶带把它贴了起来。当他抹去州座右铭时，乔治走到了车牌战争的最前线。掩盖标语违反了州法。

21
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:20.000
but a few weeks went by and not much happened. Until one day, George and Maxine were shopping. They left the store, they got to their car in the parking lot, and they saw a police officer writing them a ticket. George told me he'd been expecting this for a while. He didn't actually feel scared or surprised. Well, I was happy. You were happy?
但几个星期过去了，并没有发生什么大事。直到有一天，乔治和玛克辛在购物。他们离开商店，走到停车场他们的车前，看到一个警察正在给他们开罚单。乔治告诉我，他预料到会有这一天已经有一段时间了。他其实并不感到害怕或惊讶。嗯，我很高兴。你很高兴？

22
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:50.000
Yeah, because I was expressing my belief, my rejection of something. George refused to pay the $25 ticket, and of course, I kept the tape on, I did it again. The tickets piled up until his consistent refusal to pay landed him in court. And the judge put him away for 15 days. And so if you don't want to live free or die, you'll go to jail in New Hampshire. Two weeks may not seem like hard time, but the sentence had a huge impact on George's life.
是的，因为我在表达我的信念，表达我对某种东西的拒绝。乔治拒绝支付25美元的罚单，当然，我一直贴着胶带，我又做了一次。罚单堆积如山，直到他持续拒绝付款把他送上了法庭。法官判了他15天监禁。所以如果你不想“不自由，毋宁死”，你在新罕布什尔州就会进监狱。两周时间可能看起来不算漫长的铁窗生涯，但这次判决对乔治的生活产生了巨大影响。

